9. Launch, links and risks\par
9.1 Pre-launch checklist\par
\bullet  Price sheet and defaults complete, with every default verified against a named source\par
\bullet  All ten launch pages written, audited with 8.8 and read by a human\par
\bullet  Calculator tests pass, and each calculator works on a phone and with the keyboard only\par
\bullet  Privacy policy, terms, disclosure, about, contact, author page and methodology page live\par
\bullet  Search Console and GA4 connected, sitemap submitted\par
\bullet  Ad slots reserved, ads off until approved\par
\bullet  Lead module off, with no consent text or network details configured yet\par
\bullet  Publish all ten launch pages together, then add 2 to 3 pages a week following Section 4\par
9.2 Link plan\par
Aim for about 10 referring domains per calculator page. The benchmark ranks #1 with 6 to 9.\par
\bullet  Pitch each calculator to home improvement and DIY blogs, local contractor sites and landscaping suppliers that lack their own tools, and offer an embed with an attribution link.\par
\bullet  Offer the calculators to state and regional asphalt and paving associations as a member resource. The benchmark site belongs to one.\par
\bullet  Answer journalist requests about driveway costs through expert-quote services (check which are active in 2026).\par
\bullet  Publish an original data page only when it is built from your own sourced price sheet.\par
\bullet  Do not buy link packages. The sites in your SERP exports show anchors such as 'high quality dofollow backlinks' coming from unrelated shop and casino domains, which look like automated spam.\par
9.3 Lead network application checklist\par
\bullet  Apply to Lead Genitel, Service Direct, BuyerLink, eLocal and Home Solutions. Each lists home improvement.\par
\bullet  Ask each one for: the payout per paving, concrete, gravel and driveway repair lead or call; ZIP coverage; whether publishers outside the US are accepted; payout methods (ACH implies a US bank account, so ask about wire and other options); traffic and content rules; required consent wording; the integration method (API, postback or call tracking); and how rejected leads are reported.\par
\bullet  Keep the lead module off until you have the answers in writing.\par
9.4 Risks\par
\bullet  18 of the 20 cost keywords in your Bulk Analysis show an AI Overview, so expect lower click-through on cost pages. The benchmark calculator page still draws about 4.1K visits a month with an AI Overview present on figuring asphalt.\par
\bullet  Tools earn little per visit (average CPC 0.52 USD on the calculator keywords) and lead revenue stays unproven until a network confirms payouts. Treat every earlier revenue range as an assumption.\par
\bullet  Google's spam policies target scaled content that adds little value. Every page needs a working tool or sourced data, human review and a methodology page. Do not publish ChatGPT text that was written without data.\par
\bullet  A single lead network is a single point of failure, so apply to several.\par
\bullet  Aggregate calculators can dilute the driveway focus, so keep the bridge sections.\par
\bullet  Pages competing with the top-three incumbents (calculator.net, Inch Calculator, Omni) will not rank early. Judge them at positions 4 to 8.\par
9.5 Decision gates (not forecasts)\par
\bullet  Day 30: all launch pages indexed, and each calculator matches 10 hand-checked examples.\par
\bullet  Day 90: at least 3 launch pages in the top 20 for their primary keyword. If none, review the SERPs and links before adding pages.\par
\bullet  Network approval: turn the lead module on only with written terms, then run a 2-week test on a small share of traffic and compare leads accepted with leads submitted.\par
\bullet  Day 180: compare revenue per 1,000 visits by page type and build more of the type that earns.\par
9.6 Exit readiness\par
Keep the code and content owned or properly licensed, document each formula and the update procedure, and track revenue and accepted leads separately. AdSense accounts are generally not transferable, so confirm Google's current terms and plan the handover around the buyer's own ad setup.\par
