10.2 Compare-surfaces estimator spec (Seq 42)\par
One set of measurements returns a low-to-high cost for each surface side by side. It feeds the comparison and pillar pages and is the strongest place for the quote CTA.\par
Inputs. Area (several sections allowed), cost area tier (low, average, high), extras (remove old surface, base preparation, delivery), surfaces to compare (asphalt, concrete, stamped concrete, gravel, pavers, tar and chip), and one radio button for the surface the visitor is most interested in.\par
Outputs per surface. Low and high total, lifespan range in years, annual upkeep items, and cost per year of life (low and high). The default sort is lowest upfront cost, with a toggle to sort by cost per year.\par
Total for surface s, low and high:\par
T_s = A \\cdot u_s \\cdot t + e_s\par
Cost per year of life:\par
C_s = \\frac\{T_s\}\{L_s\}\par
A is area in square feet, u_s is the unit price per square foot from /data/prices.json (low and high), t is the cost area tier multiplier, e_s is the extras for that surface, and L_s is the lifespan in years from /data/prices.json.\par
Rules.\par
\bullet  Show a range for every surface, never a single price, and label every result as an estimate, not a quote.\par
\bullet  Cost per year stays hidden for any surface whose lifespan is null.\par
\bullet  Show the assumptions under the table: tier, extras included, and the source name and date for each price.\par
\bullet  The quote CTA prefills the radio-button surface and the area from the estimator.\par
\bullet  Share link: encode the inputs in the URL query string so a result can be sent to someone else. Add a print stylesheet so the result prints as a one-page summary.\par
\bullet  The SERPs differ: driveway paving cost calculator matches the asphalt estimator SERP, so that term stays on Seq 4 and this page keeps the surface-agnostic terms.\par
Data to add to the price sheet in 7.1. Lifespan range in years per surface, annual upkeep cost per surface, and which extras apply to which surface. Record the source name and retrieval date for each, and leave each value null until sourced.\par
