Shape and slope formulas. A tapered driveway has start width w1 and end width w2. If the visitor measures the length along the slope, use it as entered. If the visitor measures a flat distance and gives a grade, adjust the length.\par
A = L \\cdot \\frac\{w_1 + w_2\}\{2\}\par
L_s = L_f \\cdot \\sqrt\{1 + \\left(\\frac\{g\}\{100\}\\right)^2\}\par
L is length, L_f is the flat distance, L_s is the length along the slope and g is the grade in percent.\par
Delivery logic. Loads equal tons divided by truck capacity, rounded up. A short-load fee applies when a concrete order is below the supplier minimum in cubic yards. Every fee and minimum comes from /data/prices.json and stays null until sourced.\par
Worked examples. Generate every worked example by calling the calculator function with the example inputs, and add a test that fails if the page text differs from the function output. One competitor example lists 155 bags of 80 lb for about 5.2 cubic yards, which looks about 40 percent low if concrete weighs about 4,050 lb per cubic yard (the figure on Concrete Tools).\par
Density and citations. Omni assumes 1.25 metric tonnes per cubic meter for gravel, about 1.05 short tons per cubic yard, so defaults differ by source. Keep every density editable and name its source. Concrete Tools cites ACI 318 for a 10 percent waste buffer, and I cannot confirm that standard sets a waste allowance, so never cite a standard you have not read.\par
