10.7 Keyword coverage rules\par
Every page uses the exact words of each keyword in its row, once or twice, in a natural place. Google matches meaning as well as words, so this helps clarity, snippet matching and long-tail reach, and it does not guarantee rankings.\par
Where each keyword goes\par
\bullet  Primary keyword: title tag, H1, first sentence, one H2 and the meta description.\par
\bullet  Fold-in keywords: question headings, table row labels, worked examples, the Before you order checklist, image alt text and the sentences around sibling links.\par
\bullet  Question keywords become question headings, and the sentence under each one answers directly.\par
\bullet  Merged names appear once in a sentence that explains them. On the asphalt calculator, say that some countries call asphalt bitumen, so the page also works as a bitumen calculator. On the gravel calculator, say it also works as a stone calculator and a crushed stone calculator. On the rock calculator, cover river rock and landscape rock.\par
Limits\par
\bullet  No exact keyword more than 4 times on a page. This is an editorial guideline, not a Google rule.\par
\bullet  If an exact keyword is ungrammatical, such as how much for a asphalt driveway, use a natural close variant and note it in the coverage report.\par
\bullet  Never write a sentence only to hold a keyword.\par
\bullet  Cement driveway wording follows the Seq 5 rule in 10.3.\par
Checks. Each page lists its keywords in frontmatter, the build fails when one is missing, and prompt 8.7 prints a coverage report that the 8.8 audit then checks.\par
